  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nAn upper-level low over the northern Plains today will slowly\nmove east southeast into the Ohio Valley through Wednesday.\nDisturbances in the cyclonic flow to its south will bring a\nchance of showers to southeast Missouri this afternoon through\nearly Tuesday. With some lingering moisture this afternoon, \ninstability is building over the Ozark Foothills and a few \nthunderstorms will be possible. Very localized gusty winds will \nbe possible to go along with lightning and brief heavy \ndownpours. Any instability will be lost with sunset, so no \nthunder is expected tonight.\n\nCool, surface high pressure will continue to build across the\nQuad State, and there will be plentiful cloud cover, especially\nacross the northern half of the area until the upper low passes\nto the east of the area Wednesday. High pressure aloft will then\nbuild overhead Thursday into Friday and possibly through the\nweekend, while surface high pressure remains in entrenched over\nthe region. This will produce some gorgeous fall conditions.\n\nAt some point over the weekend, enough energy may impinge on \nthe upper ridge to bring a small chance of showers or \nthunderstorms most likely to southeast Missouri and southern \nIllinois, but the details are too murky at this time to get \nexcited about any rainfall.",
